Verification
------------

Run from the repo root after the build:

```sh
READELF="$ANDROID_NDK_ROOT/toolchains/llvm/prebuilt/darwin-x86_64/bin/llvm-readelf"
for lib in android/libs/*/libavutil*.so; do echo "$lib"; strings "$lib" | grep "license:"; strings "$lib" | grep -- "--prefix="; done
for lib in android/libs/*/libavfilter*.so; do echo "$lib $(strings "$lib" | grep -ci rubberband)"; done
for lib in android/libs/*/*.so; do echo "$lib $("$READELF" -lW "$lib" | awk '/LOAD/{print $NF}' | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"; done
ls android/ffmpeg-kit-android-lib/src/main/res/raw
git diff --exit-code android/ffmpeg-kit-android-lib/build.gradle
```

Expected result:

- every `libavutil.so`, and armeabi-v7a's `libavutil_neon.so`, reports
  `libavutil license: LGPL version 2.1 or later`
- each configuration line has `--enable-libmp3lame`, `--enable-libopus`,
  `--enable-libvorbis` and `--enable-iconv`, and has neither `--enable-gpl`,
  `--enable-version3` nor `--enable-librubberband`
- each `libavfilter*.so` rubberband count is 0
- every `.so` shows only `0x4000`, the 16 KB alignment of its `LOAD` segments
- `res/raw` holds exactly `license.txt` (FFmpegKit's LGPL 3.0 `LICENSE`),
  `source.txt`, `license_cpu_features.txt`, `license_lame.txt`,
  `license_libiconv.txt`, `license_libogg.txt`, `license_libvorbis.txt` and
  `license_opus.txt`; the build clears the folder before copying
- `build.gradle` has no diff

Before uploading a release, check that its bundle packages the rebuilt FFmpeg;
the line reads `LGPL version 2.1 or later`:

```sh
unzip -p <release-bundle>.aab base/lib/arm64-v8a/libavutil.so | strings | grep "license:"
```

Notes
-----

- `src/*` contains downloaded/upstream nested repositories used by the build.
  They are not part of the top-level repo history.
- The build generates outputs under `.tmp/`, `prebuilt/`, `android/libs/`,
  `android/obj/`, and `android/ffmpeg-kit-android-lib/src/main/res/raw/`.
- Some IDEs may still show generated files or nested repos under `src/*`.
- `tools/android/.gradle/` is local IDE/Gradle cache noise.
- If switching machines or build paths, forcing rebuild of the enabled
  dependencies is safer than trying to reuse old `prebuilt/*` metadata.
